[1mBug 1: [38;5;10mfgets_line[0m didn't emulate C's [38;5;10mfgets[0m buffer truncation[22m (affected: [38;5;10mtoken_255_chars[0m, [38;5;10mlong_pattern[0m, [38;5;10mnull_byte[0m)[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The original Rust used [38;5;10mstdin.lock().read_line()[0m which reads an entire line with no size limit. In C, [38;5;10mfgets(buf, sizeof(buf), stdin)[0m reads at most [38;5;10msizeof(buf) - 1[0m bytes and stops — leaving the remainder in the stream for the next read. This matters when input lines exceed 255 chars (the C [38;5;10mchar input[256][0m / [38;5;10mchar line[256][0m buffers). Additionally, C's [38;5;10mstrlen[0m/[38;5;10mstrncat[0m stop at null bytes, but Rust strings include them.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Fix in [38;5;10mmain.rs[0m: Replaced [38;5;10mfgets_line[0m with a byte-level [38;5;10mfgets(stdin, buf_size)[0m that reads at most [38;5;10mbuf_size - 1[0m bytes, stops at [38;5;10m\n[0m or EOF, and truncates at the first null byte.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mBug 2: Menu parsing used [38;5;10mstr::parse[0m instead of [38;5;10msscanf[0m semantics[22m (affected: [38;5;10mnested_string_escapes[0m)[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust code used [38;5;10minput.trim().parse::<i32>()[0m which requires the [3mentire[23m string to be a valid integer. C's [38;5;10msscanf(input, "%d", &choice)[0m extracts the first integer from the beginning and ignores trailing garbage. When stdin contains no real newlines (e.g., literal [38;5;10m\n[0m escape sequences), the entire input is read as one line, and [38;5;10mparse()[0m fails on [38;5;10m"1\n\"He said..."[0m while [38;5;10msscanf[0m successfully extracts [38;5;10m1[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Fix in [38;5;10mmain.rs[0m: Added [38;5;10msscanf_int()[0m that extracts a leading integer from the string, ignoring trailing content.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mBug 3: [38;5;10mfind_patterns[0m rejected empty patterns[22m (affected: [38;5;10mempty_at_prompts[0m, [38;5;10mempty_pattern[0m)[0m[0m
